LYNX.EXE: Lynx 2.9.3, the text web browser, for DOS on vmdos's own TCP.

    STARTNET                         (once: the TCP/IP settings, by DHCP)
    LYNX                             (the start page: LYNX.CFG's STARTFILE)
    LYNX https://example.com/
    LYNX -dump https://example.com/ > PAGE.TXT     (the page as text)
    LYNX -source https://example.com/ > PAGE.HTM   (the HTML as it came)
    LYNX C:\DOCS\INDEX.HTM           (local files and folders too)

Keys are Lynx's: arrows move between links and follow them (Right/Enter)
and go back (Left), G opens a URL, / searches, Space and B page, Q quits,
H is the help (on lynx.invisible-island.net), O the options, A bookmarks,
D downloads a link to a file, \ shows the HTML. Z or Ctrl-G stops a page
that's loading.

The mouse (vmdos's own, or CTMOUSE): click a link to follow it. On the
bottom line the left end goes back, the next bit forward, the right end
cancels, the middle pages down; on the top line the middle pages up.
USE_MOUSE:FALSE in LYNX.CFG for keys only.

Built from:
  Lynx       github.com/ThomasDickey/lynx-snapshots, the commit labelled
             v2-9-3 (Lynx 2.9.3, May 2026), GPL-2.0, with lynx-dos.patch;
             configured for DJGPP with OpenSSL, IPv6, zlib, nested tables,
             source cache, persistent cookies
  OpenSSL    3.6.5 (tools/build-openssl.sh, third_party/openssl): HTTPS,
             TLS 1.2 and 1.3, Lynx's own OpenSSL code
  PDCurses   github.com/wmcbrine/PDCurses 3.9, its DOS port (BIOS, the
             text screen, INT 33h for the mouse), public domain
  zlib       github.com/madler/zlib 1.3.1: gzip/deflate encoded pages
DJGPP gcc 12 (32-bit, needs DPMI: vmdos's own), UPX --best.

The files here:
  lynx-dos.patch     the changes to Lynx: the mouse with PDCurses under
                     DJGPP (Lynx left it out: "PDCurses' mouse code does
                     nothing in the DJGPP configuration", true of PDCurses
                     2.x in 2000); its DOS idle loop, which waited for a key
                     only, wakes up for a mouse button too
  lynx-dos.h         forced into every Lynx file: Lynx's calls to connect,
                     read_s, close_s and SSL_read go to lynx-dos.c; the
                     clock is ossl-dos.c's (../openssl/dos-time.h); what
                     Lynx's (Windows) PDCurses mouse code wants
  lynx-dos.c         Lynx's part of the network glue on top of
                     ../openssl/ossl-dos.c (the Watt-32 calls Lynx's DJGPP
                     port makes, shared with OPENSSL.EXE): connect() as
                     Lynx's non-blocking loop wants it (EINPROGRESS,
                     EALREADY, EISCONN); a read the user stops returns
                     HT_INTERRUPTED; the mouse for the idle loop; HOME and
                     TEMP when DOS has none; "run STARTNET" as an alert
  LYNX.CFG           the settings (C:\VMDOS\NET\LYNX.CFG): start page, help
                     page, code page 437 for the screen, cookies, EDIT as
                     the editor, timeouts, the mouse

Networking is ssh's and WGET's (third_party/openssh/README.vmdos): vmdos
runs the TCP connections itself on the PC's network card, IPv4 and IPv6;
names are looked up with the DNS servers in VMTCP.CFG. No packet driver or
DOS TCP/IP stack, nothing in vmdos's kernel. Without the TCP/IP settings
(no STARTNET yet) Lynx shows an alert saying so, and local files still work.

HTTPS is OpenSSL's, through Lynx's own OpenSSL code: certificates are
checked against %SSL_CERT_FILE%, C:\VMDOS\NET\CACERT.PEM unless set (the CAs
WGET uses), and the host name; when that fails Lynx asks ("SSL error:...
-Continue?"; FORCE_SSL_PROMPT in LYNX.CFG or -noprompt change that).

Files. LYNX.CFG next to LYNX.EXE (C:\VMDOS\NET), or LYNX_CFG=file, or
LYNX -cfg=file. Lynx's own lynx.cfg (in its source) lists every setting.
HOME is C:\HOME unless set (bookmarks: lynx_bookmarks.html, cookies:
.lynx_cookies; long names: DOSLFN, which AUTOEXEC.BAT loads); temporary
files go to %TEMP%, else C:\TEMP.

Not there: FTP's active mode (no listening sockets; Lynx uses passive), the
help pages on disk (H shows them from the web).

Build: tools/build-lynx.sh writes bin/LYNX.EXE (the normal build doesn't
need it: the binary is committed); the Makefile puts it and LYNX.CFG in
C:\VMDOS\NET when it's there. It needs DJGPP (a cross gcc
i586-pc-msdosdjgpp-gcc on PATH, or DJGPP=dir), git, make, perl, a host gcc
with -m32, python3 and upx; it fetches Lynx, OpenSSL (through
tools/build-openssl.sh), PDCurses, zlib and Watt-32 (only its headers;
none of its code is linked) at the pinned versions. Lynx's configure runs
as a cross build with the glue standing in for Watt-32; then lynx-dos.h
goes into every file.

Found on the way: Lynx's restart_curses() (for "default colors") wants
ncurses, so that's off (DOS has no default colors anyway); color styles
(LYNX.LSS) are off too: Lynx's classic colors (COLOR: in LYNX.CFG).
